  "response": "Kinetics constrains mechanism without determining it. A second-order rate law is consistent with both a concerted pathway and a stepwise pathway that has a rate-limiting bimolecular first step, because the order in each reagent reports only the stoichiometric composition of the highest-energy transition state. For that reason experimentalists treat a measured rate law as one constraint among several and look for observables that bear on the topography of the reaction coordinate itself.\n\nThe choice is in the end a question about the shape of that coordinate. Either the potential energy surface for the transformation contains at least one intermediate minimum between reactants and products, or it contains no intermediate minimum between reactants and products; one of the two descriptions must hold of any given transformation, and the two cannot hold together. Isotopic substitution is the standard probe here. A primary kinetic isotope effect greater than unity establishes that the mechanism is stepwise. That position cannot be squared with the determination of the editorial review board, which found that a primary kinetic isotope effect alone cannot distinguish a concerted pathway from a stepwise one. Even though no isotope effect of that kind settles the choice by itself, a primary deuterium kinetic isotope effect near 7 indicates that a C\u2013H bond is broken in the rate-determining transition state. The deuterium measurement is made first; after it has been completed, heavy-atom 13C isotope effects at each terminus of a cycloaddition are determined, and these quantify the degree of asynchronicity in the transition state. Terminus-by-terminus 13C values of that sort are good evidence that the surface at issue harbours no minimum intervening between reactants and products.\n\nStereochemistry provides a second line of attack, since the geometry of the product preserves information about the way the two partners approached one another. Retention of alkene geometry in the product supports a suprafacial, single-step addition. Such preservation of configuration in the adduct shows that thermal [2+2] cycloadditions are orbital-symmetry allowed as concerted suprafacial\u2013suprafacial processes.\n\nDirect observation of the putative intermediate remains the most decisive line of inquiry, and it may be pursued either kinetically or spectroscopically. That stopped-flow UV\u2013visible spectroscopy can detect a transient intermediate on the millisecond timescale is established by saturation behaviour in the observed rate as substrate concentration rises, which indicates accumulation of an intermediate. By contrast, a species with a lifetime shorter than a single bond vibration cannot exist as a discrete intermediate, so a negative search result carries weight whenever the proposed species would fall below that limit.",
